FINDINGS:
1. [BLOCKER] src/interpreter.rs:2119 — `random.seed(1)` (and `random.number/choice/shuffle`) answers though no `random` module exists — the `call_method` fall-through calls `qualified` whenever it is registered regardless of whether the receiver is a module, and `random` passes the analyzer because `src/analyzer.rs:216` treats every builtin as bound — the matrix in tests/stdlib_module_names_test.rs:777 only walks `MODULES`, so non-module receivers are never asked about — add a receiver check (refuse when the resolved module is not in `MODULES`/imports/declared) or register a `random` module decision explicitly; settle by running `say random.seed(1)` (predict: answers instead of refusing).
2. [MAJOR] SPEC.md:1056 — claims ``length` answers a text, a list or a record alike`` but `src/runtime.rs:788` answers only list/text and errors `length requires a list or text` (same in `src/stdlib.rs:615`), contradicting `tests/test_text.rb:132` and `corpus/stdlib-0005.expected` which pin record-length as a caught error — correct the sentence to text-or-list only.
3. [MAJOR] src/interpreter.rs:2108 — the new early `stdlib::is_module && !module_has_member` refuse fires before consulting declared/imported modules, so a program declaring `module text ... end` with its own member (allowed by `declare_module` at src/interpreter.rs:1056, which only rejects duplicates) is refused as `Module 'text' has no function` even though `text_greet` is published (same hole at src/bytecode/vm.rs:2757) — scope the check to non-declared modules or check `get_var`/declared table first; no test declares a stdlib-colliding module name.
